      "id": 2,
      "claim": "This gravitational force provides the inward (centripetal) acceleration required for Earth's orbital motion.",
      "evidence": "For circular motion a_c = v^2 / r; using Earth's orbital speed (~30 km/s) and r gives a_c ≈ 0.006 m/s², equal to the Sun's gravitational acceleration at 1 AU.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Because the force continuously changes Earth's velocity direction, Earth follows an elliptical (approximately circular) path around the Sun rather than a straight line.",
      "evidence": "Newton's first law: an object moves straight unless acted on by a net force; a constant central force yields a curved trajectory (Kepler's first law).",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Earth's inertia supplies the tangential component of motion while the Sun's gravity supplies the radial component, together producing a stable orbit.",
      "evidence": "Orbital dynamics: balance of centripetal (gravitational) force and the object's tendency to move straight (inertia) results in a closed orbit.",
      "weight": 0.05,
      "depends_on": [1, 2, 3]
    }
